  "notes": [
    "The header is the same as the Pico 1: same 40 pins, same positions, same GP numbers. A carrier board or a HAT built for a Pico takes a Pico 2 without changes.",
    "You pick the architecture at build time. The RP2350 carries two Cortex-M33 cores and two Hazard3 RISC-V cores on the same die, and the bootloader selects which pair runs. Only one pair at a time.",
    "520 KB of SRAM against the Pico 1's 264 KB, 4 MB of flash against 2 MB, and 12 PIO state machines across three blocks against 8 across two.",
    "PWM slice numbering does not carry over. The RP2350 has 12 slices where the RP2040 had 8, so the slice and channel a given GPIO lands on differs above GP15. Let the SDK work it out rather than porting hard-coded slice numbers.",
    "Still 3.3 V and still not 5 V tolerant.",
    "Peripheral mappings are not fixed: each pin lists the SPI, I2C or UART instance it can reach and you choose in software. Two pins on one instance cannot serve the same role at once.",
    "ADC_VREF is a filtered 3.3 V reference and AGND is the analog return. Use those for analog work rather than a digital ground pin.",
    "The RP2350 A2 stepping has a documented errata on GPIO input latching when a pin is switched from output to input with a pull-down. If an input reads stuck high, check the errata before blaming your wiring.",
    "On the Pico 2 W the wireless chip takes GP23, GP24, GP25 and GP29 internally, exactly as on the Pico W. None of those are on the header anyway."
  ],
